🤖 [Qwen Edge] Certainly! Below is a simple and efficient way to reverse a Go slice in place:

```go
package main

import "fmt"

func reverseInPlace(slice []int) {
    n := len(slice)
    for i, j := 0, n-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
        slice[i], slice[j] = slice[j], slice[i]
    }
}

func main() {
    var s []int
    // Initialize your slice here

    reverseInPlace(s)

    fmt.Println("Reversed:", s)
}
```

This script defines a function `reverseInPlace` that takes an integer array (`[]int`) and reverses its elements in place. It uses two pointers, one starting at the beginning of the slice (`i`) and one at the end (`j`). The elements are swapped as they go from the start to the middle.

To test this script, you can initialize a slice (e.g., `s := []int{1, 2, 3, 4}`) before calling `reverseInPlace`, and then print out the reversed slice.

