```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Define a Node class for holding each element in the linked list.
class ListNode {
public:
    int data;
    ListNode* next;

    // Constructor to initialize the node with some data, but empty next pointer initially.
    ListNode(int val) : data(val), next(nullptr) {}
};

// This function creates and initializes an array of nodes.
ListNode** createList() {
    ListNode* head = new ListNode(1);  // Initialize the linked list with a header node (dummy node)

    if (!head) return nullptr;  // If there was not enough memory to allocate for a header node, then we are out of memory.

    // Create nodes and attach them in order
    for(int i = 2; i < 10; ++i) {
        ListNode* newnode = new ListNode(i);  // Create the next node.
        newnode->next = head->next;  // Make sure to point this node at the header node (which is already pointing to our first element)
        head->next = newnode;
    }

    return &head;  // Return address of the header node
}

// This function traverses the list and prints each data value.
void traverseList(ListNode* head) {
    while(head != nullptr) {
        cout << head->data << " ";
        head = head->next;
    }
    cout << endl;
}

int main() {
    ListNode** phead = createList();

    if (!phead) return 1;  // If there was not enough memory, then we have failed.

    traverseList(*phead);  // Print the list

    delete[] *phead;  // Free the memory allocated for each node
    return 0;
}
```

In this implementation:
- We define a `ListNode` class to represent nodes in our linked list. Each node contains an integer data and a pointer to the next node.
- The `createList` function allocates dynamic memory (using `new`) to create a header node which points to the first element of our array of nodes. 
- The second parameter of `createList` is returned by the function, so we can pass this address back to the caller and deallocate it when needed.
- In `traverseList`, we traverse from the header node (which points to the first node in the list) until we reach a null pointer which indicates the end of the list. We print each data value on separate lines.
- The `main` function creates an array of nodes, prints them out, and then cleans up by freeing the allocated memory.

